{
  "note": [
    "The shared map-fanout transition table. One artefact, two runners, so the",
    "Rust and TypeScript map engines cannot drift apart silently.",
    "",
    "`transitions` are single-step engine cases: descriptor state plus one",
    "event in, an ordered effect list or a rejection out. The TypeScript runner",
    "(packages/core/test/map-engine.test.ts) drives them straight through",
    "map-engine.ts's `step`. The Rust engine is a private module (`mod",
    "map_engine;` in src/lib.rs, `pub(crate)` items), so an integration test in",
    "tests/ cannot call `step` at all; the Rust runner therefore asserts the",
    "`fanouts` section instead. See `rustRunnerBlocker` below.",
    "",
    "`fanouts` are scripted whole-fanout cases expressed only in terms of the",
    "public provider API — claim an item, complete it, fail it, read the",
    "parent's history. Both runtimes execute every one of them against their",
    "own providers, so this section is the part that is genuinely asserted on",
    "both sides today."
  ],
  "rustRunnerBlocker": {
    "what": "tests/map_transitions.rs cannot assert the `transitions` section.",
    "why": "src/map_engine.rs is a private module with pub(crate) items, and a Cargo integration test only sees the crate's public API.",
    "unblock": "Expose the engine to tests — `#[cfg(test)] pub use map_engine::...` or a `pub mod map_engine` behind a test-only feature in src/lib.rs — after which the Rust runner is the same table walk the TypeScript one already performs."
  },
  "exclusions": [
    {
      "what": "ScheduleItemRetry.visibleAtMs and .timeoutAtMs values",
      "why": "The two runtimes have different retry-policy models — RetryBackoff::{None,Exponential} over a fixed base in Rust, initialIntervalMs/maxIntervalMs/backoffCoefficient in TypeScript — that share no encoding, so numeric parity is impossible. Only the shape is normative and is asserted: `visibleAtMs === null` means immediately claimable, and a timed-out attempt always yields null. Cases carry `visibleAtMs` as \"Null\" or \"Deferred\" and `timeoutAtMs` as \"Null\" or \"Present\" rather than as numbers."
    },
    {
      "what": "Every DescriptorCreated case where recordedOutcomes >= itemCount, including but not limited to the empty manifest",
      "why": "TypeScript completes such a map at descriptor creation; Rust stalls, because all three Rust providers stall. The runtimes are knowingly divergent until the plan row that converges Rust lands. The exclusion is the whole predicate, not just itemCount === 0: where nextOrdinal < itemCount the TypeScript engine emits MaterializeItems and CompleteMap in one list and Rust emits only the materialization."
    },
    {
      "what": "The ParentCancelled event",
      "why": "Both runtimes now drive it from WorkflowTaskCommit::cancel_commands / cancelCommands, and both emit the same two effects, so the exclusion is no longer about reachability. It stays because the transition is only half the behaviour: cancelling a child map must also cancel the children it already started, and that is deliberately not a step effect — the other producer, a run reaching a terminal event, must leave its children to the parentClosePolicy path, which is free to abandon them. Each provider therefore pairs ParentCancelled with its own child cancellation, and a table that asserted only the effect list would pin the half that agrees and say nothing about the half that matters. Each engine's own unit tests cover the transition."
    }
  ],
